{
  "$schema_note": "FROZEN ANCHOR canonical WIRE vectors. This is the byte-for-byte broadcast payload of an ANCHOR action per protocol version. It is the consensus contract between the xchain-hub PRODUCER (StateAnchorPublisher._buildV7Payload (the v0 bundle builder; the method keeps its historical name)) and the xchain-indexer PARSER (src/actions/anchor.js formats[0] positional extraction). A one-character field-order drift between the two silently forks validator signatures (followers fail to co-sign, or recovery rebuilds divergent state) with NO build/test failure unless these vectors are asserted on both sides.",
  "authority": "xchain-documentation/protocol/test-vectors/anchor_canonical.json is authoritative. xchain-hub/test/fixtures and xchain-indexer/test/fixtures vendor byte-identical copies so each repo CI can assert without a sibling checkout. Keep all three identical.",
  "regeneration": "Regenerate ONLY on an intentional, fleet-coordinated ANCHOR wire-format change (a NEW version byte), never to make a test pass. v1/v2 (archive batch + continuation chunk) are intentionally out of scope here; they carry a compressed/base64 blob and are covered by the archive round-trip tests. v3 (the archive fold) IS in scope: it is the same section grammar as v0 plus an optional archive section, no compressed blob shape of its own to round-trip separately.",
  "field_order": {
    "v0": "ANCHOR|0|NETWORK|SNAPSHOT_BLOCK|SECTION_COUNT|{CHAIN|BLOCK_INDEX|BLOCK_HASH|LEDGER_HASH|ACTIONS_HASH|CONTRACT_HASH|CHECKPOINT_SEQ|SECTION_SNAPSHOT_BLOCK|STATE_ROOT|STATE_ROOT_VERSION|BLOCK_MERKLE_ROOT|BLOCK_MERKLE_VERSION|SIG_COUNT|(PUBKEY|SIG)...} x SECTION_COUNT|PUBLISHER|ATTEST_SIG_COUNT|(APUBKEY|ASIG)...",
    "v0_rules": "Sections ordered by CHAIN ascending (byte order). Within a section the (PUBKEY,SIG) pairs are ordered by PUBKEY ascending. The bundle SNAPSHOT_BLOCK is the MAX of the sections SECTION_SNAPSHOT_BLOCK. Each section omits NETWORK; the indexer rebuilds every section canonical (XCHECKPOINT|CHAIN|NETWORK|...) with the header NETWORK. The fixture below deliberately lists sections and one signature list OUT of order so both producer and parser tests prove they apply the ordering rules rather than echo fixture order. Retired versions 0/3/4/5 (one anchor per chain) were removed from this file on 2026-08-28 by operator ruling (pre-launch, superseded wires deleted). On 2026-08-30 the version set restarted at v0 (this bundle, formerly v7) with v1 = the archive head (formerly v6) and v2 = the continuation chunk, gated by ANCHOR_ACTIVATION; every anchor below that height is invalid.",
    "v3": "ANCHOR|3|NETWORK|SNAPSHOT_BLOCK|SECTION_COUNT|{CHAIN|BLOCK_INDEX|BLOCK_HASH|LEDGER_HASH|ACTIONS_HASH|CONTRACT_HASH|CHECKPOINT_SEQ|SECTION_SNAPSHOT_BLOCK|STATE_ROOT|STATE_ROOT_VERSION|BLOCK_MERKLE_ROOT|BLOCK_MERKLE_VERSION|SIG_COUNT|(PUBKEY|SIG)...} x SECTION_COUNT|ARCHIVE_COUNT|[WRAPPER_SECTION_INDEX|MATCH_BATCH_SEQ|MATCH_COUNT|BATCH_CRC32|TOTAL_CHUNKS|ARCHIVE_B64]|PUBLISHER|ATTEST_SIG_COUNT|(APUBKEY|ASIG)...",
    "v3_rules": "Added by the archive fold and gated by ANCHOR_FOLD_ACTIVATION (null / never-active on every network today: the wire and vectors ship ahead of the indexer parse, hub publisher fold and sdk light-client support, which ship separately). The SECTION_COUNT section block is byte-identical in shape and ordering rules to v0's. ARCHIVE_COUNT is 0 or 1 only; a value outside that range, or one that disagrees with whether an archive section is present, is invalid: ARCHIVE_COUNT. The bracketed archive fields appear ONLY when ARCHIVE_COUNT is 1. WRAPPER_SECTION_INDEX is a 0-based index into this v3's own SECTION_COUNT sections naming which chain section's signatures extend to cover the archive fields (see the wrapper_section_index fixture below); every other section signs the plain v0 section canonical. There is exactly one PUBLISHER/ATTEST_SIG_COUNT tail per v3, signing the same XANCPUB|anchor_bundle|... tuple a v0 signs, never a separate anchor_archive tuple. v0/v1/v2 above are untouched by the fold: this change only ADDS the v3 vectors below."
  },
